1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,800
Have you ever wondered how much plastic
you actually throw away? Bottles.

2
00:00:05,030 --> 00:00:07,400
Packaging. Plastic bags. Broken toys.

3
00:00:07,630 --> 00:00:09,280
All the obvious things.

4
00:00:09,977 --> 00:00:13,657
But what if some of the plastic around
you doesn't have to be thrown away at all?

5
00:00:13,957 --> 00:00:16,477
What if you can create microplastics...

6
00:00:16,617 --> 00:00:18,637
simply by using something?

7
00:00:18,827 --> 00:00:23,047
Because some of the things we use every
day are slowly wearing themselves away.

8
00:00:23,987 --> 00:00:26,767
And some of that missing
material doesn't disappear.

9
00:00:27,447 --> 00:00:29,587
It becomes tiny plastic particles.

10
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:32,513
And you may be producing them right now.

11
00:00:33,551 --> 00:00:38,251
In another video, we followed one of the
biggest sources of microplastic pollution.

12
00:00:39,091 --> 00:00:40,771
The tyres on your car.

13
00:00:41,308 --> 00:00:43,388
This time, we'll stay much closer.

14
00:00:43,588 --> 00:00:45,788
Much closer to your hands.

15
00:00:47,258 --> 00:00:51,838
A kitchen sponge. You use it to remove
food from a plate. You squeeze it.

16
00:00:51,918 --> 00:00:53,758
You scrub with it. You rinse it.

17
00:00:53,911 --> 00:00:55,531
And every time you do that...

18
00:00:55,611 --> 00:00:57,911
the sponge itself is being worn away.

19
00:00:58,572 --> 00:01:04,792
A 2026 study measured microplastic release
from kitchen sponges during normal use.

20
00:01:05,388 --> 00:01:09,528
Depending on the type of sponge, the
researchers estimated roughly zero

21
00:01:09,758 --> 00:01:14,708
point seven to four point two grams
of microplastics per person per year.

22
00:01:14,933 --> 00:01:16,673
That doesn't sound like much.

23
00:01:16,753 --> 00:01:19,373
Until you remember what is happening.

24
00:01:20,045 --> 00:01:22,205
You are using an object
made partly of plastic...

25
00:01:22,435 --> 00:01:24,385
to clean your dishes...

26
00:01:24,705 --> 00:01:27,265
and some of that object
is going down the drain.

27
00:01:27,606 --> 00:01:29,766
And ordinary kitchen sponges aren't the

28
00:01:29,996 --> 00:01:32,586
only cleaning products
that behave this way.

29
00:01:33,142 --> 00:01:37,762
Melamine cleaning sponges, the
famous white "magic erasers", can

30
00:01:37,992 --> 00:01:41,982
produce millions of microscopic
polymer fibres as they wear down.

31
00:01:42,622 --> 00:01:47,522
That means about six and a half million
fibres for every gram that wears away.

32
00:01:48,152 --> 00:01:50,552
The sponge isn't just
cleaning the surface.

33
00:01:51,272 --> 00:01:53,712
The sponge is also slowly disappearing.

34
00:01:54,501 --> 00:01:57,981
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

35
00:01:58,601 --> 00:02:00,201
It really helps the channel.

36
00:02:02,591 --> 00:02:05,431
Now let's move from the
sink to the kitchen counter.

37
00:02:05,571 --> 00:02:07,791
Because there is another object that can

38
00:02:08,021 --> 00:02:10,631
put plastic surprisingly
close to your food.

39
00:02:11,307 --> 00:02:13,087
A plastic cutting board.

40
00:02:13,607 --> 00:02:17,107
Every time a knife hits the board,
it doesn't only cut the food.

41
00:02:17,767 --> 00:02:19,647
It also scratches the board.

42
00:02:20,325 --> 00:02:22,125
The knife creates grooves.

43
00:02:22,205 --> 00:02:24,205
The surface becomes rougher.

44
00:02:24,285 --> 00:02:27,525
And tiny pieces of the
polymer can be removed.

45
00:02:28,297 --> 00:02:31,297
Researchers have measured
microplastics released from both

46
00:02:31,527 --> 00:02:35,497
polyethylene and polypropylene
cutting boards during chopping.

47
00:02:35,779 --> 00:02:37,979
And newer experiments
have even detected plastic

48
00:02:38,209 --> 00:02:42,039
particles transferred from
worn cutting boards onto meat.

49
00:02:42,638 --> 00:02:46,718
So when you see thousands of little
knife marks on a plastic cutting board...

50
00:02:46,958 --> 00:02:49,798
you're looking at something
more than cosmetic damage.

51
00:02:50,521 --> 00:02:54,021
You're looking at evidence that the
surface has been physically removed.

52
00:02:56,761 --> 00:03:00,201
But the kitchen has one
more trick. The dishwasher.

53
00:03:00,976 --> 00:03:05,276
We normally think of a dishwasher as
a machine that just cleans the dishes.

54
00:03:05,643 --> 00:03:09,903
But the cleaning process involves hot
water, detergents, jets of water...

55
00:03:09,983 --> 00:03:11,603
and mechanical forces.

56
00:03:12,395 --> 00:03:16,135
Researchers have now tested
plastic containers and utensils

57
00:03:16,365 --> 00:03:20,155
made from several common
polymers in domestic dishwashers.

58
00:03:20,725 --> 00:03:23,125
They found that mechanical dishwashing can

59
00:03:23,355 --> 00:03:26,645
release both microplastics
and nanoplastics.

60
00:03:27,136 --> 00:03:30,596
In other words... the thing you put
into the dishwasher to get clean...

61
00:03:30,836 --> 00:03:33,376
can itself shed material
during the process.

62
00:03:34,608 --> 00:03:38,068
And the dishwasher has a
neighbour. Your washing machine.

63
00:03:38,845 --> 00:03:43,105
You probably already know that synthetic
clothes can release microfibres.

64
00:03:43,505 --> 00:03:46,965
Polyester. Nylon. Acrylic.

65
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:50,050
These materials are made from polymers.

66
00:03:50,310 --> 00:03:52,610
And when you wash them,
the fabric is repeatedly

67
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:55,810
stretched, bent and rubbed against itself.

68
00:03:56,472 --> 00:03:58,412
Tiny fibres can break away.

69
00:03:58,492 --> 00:04:00,572
And they leave with the wastewater.

70
00:04:01,626 --> 00:04:04,086
Researchers have been
studying this for years.

71
00:04:04,166 --> 00:04:07,506
But different studies get
very different numbers.

72
00:04:08,188 --> 00:04:11,808
The fabric matters. So does the
machine, the washing programme...

73
00:04:12,038 --> 00:04:15,388
and even the way researchers
count the fibres.

74
00:04:15,559 --> 00:04:17,479
So there isn't one magic number.

75
00:04:17,559 --> 00:04:20,199
But the basic phenomenon is clear.

76
00:04:20,839 --> 00:04:23,969
Washing synthetic textiles
can release microfibres.

77
00:04:24,119 --> 00:04:27,019
And those fibres can end up in wastewater.

78
00:04:30,089 --> 00:04:31,909
And now let's leave the house.

79
00:04:31,989 --> 00:04:36,289
Because one of the strangest
examples is waiting in your garden.

80
00:04:36,929 --> 00:04:41,989
The string trimmer. You probably think
of the spinning nylon line as a tool.

81
00:04:42,209 --> 00:04:45,249
But it is also something
that gets used up.

82
00:04:45,818 --> 00:04:47,778
It hits grass. It hits weeds.

83
00:04:47,858 --> 00:04:50,258
It hits stones. It hits fences.

84
00:04:50,743 --> 00:04:54,943
And as it hits those things, some of
the line wears away or breaks off.

85
00:04:55,553 --> 00:04:59,233
Researchers actually tested what
happens during this process.

86
00:05:00,073 --> 00:05:02,453
They estimated that
thousands of microplastic

87
00:05:02,683 --> 00:05:04,933
particles are released every minute.

88
00:05:05,507 --> 00:05:08,787
But the really surprising
part was the nanoplastics.

89
00:05:09,465 --> 00:05:12,765
The researchers estimated that the
trimmer could release billions of

90
00:05:12,995 --> 00:05:17,125
nanoscale plastic particles per
minute under their test conditions.

91
00:05:17,966 --> 00:05:19,986
Billions. From a machine
whose job is simply...

92
00:05:20,216 --> 00:05:21,646
cutting grass.

93
00:05:22,393 --> 00:05:25,733
This isn't plastic pollution
from throwing something away.

94
00:05:26,193 --> 00:05:28,893
The plastic is being
used exactly as intended.

95
00:05:31,483 --> 00:05:34,023
The same basic thing
happens under your feet.

96
00:05:34,103 --> 00:05:35,663
Your shoes.

97
00:05:36,381 --> 00:05:39,441
Most people think of a shoe
as leather, fabric, rubber...

98
00:05:39,521 --> 00:05:41,521
something that protects your feet.

99
00:05:41,915 --> 00:05:44,735
But modern soles often
contain synthetic polymers

100
00:05:44,815 --> 00:05:47,455
such as polyurethane and synthetic rubber.

101
00:05:48,035 --> 00:05:49,815
And soles wear down.

102
00:05:50,397 --> 00:05:52,117
Every step creates friction.

103
00:05:52,857 --> 00:05:57,557
A little material disappears. Then
another little bit. And another.

104
00:05:58,135 --> 00:06:03,255
Researchers have measured microplastic
particles produced by footwear abrasion.

105
00:06:03,841 --> 00:06:07,261
In outdoor environments,
particles from shoe soles have

106
00:06:07,491 --> 00:06:11,461
even been detected alongside
fibres from synthetic clothing.

107
00:06:12,129 --> 00:06:14,609
So every step is a tiny wear event.

108
00:06:14,789 --> 00:06:17,449
Usually far too small to notice.

109
00:06:17,801 --> 00:06:20,181
But multiply it by thousands of steps.

110
00:06:20,261 --> 00:06:22,261
Millions of people.

111
00:06:22,341 --> 00:06:24,121
And years of use.

112
00:06:24,441 --> 00:06:28,071
And suddenly the missing material
becomes a source of pollution.

113
00:06:30,721 --> 00:06:35,261
And then there is something even
closer to your body. Your toothbrush.

114
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:37,720
You use it twice a day.

115
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:40,280
The bristles bend thousands of times.

116
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:42,040
They rub against your teeth.

117
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:44,000
They rub against each other.

118
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:46,320
And eventually they become worn.

119
00:06:46,985 --> 00:06:51,685
A 2025 study simulated normal
brushing with commercial toothbrushes

120
00:06:51,915 --> 00:06:56,385
and found microplastic particles
released from their bristles.

121
00:06:56,787 --> 00:06:59,347
The exact amount varied between products.

122
00:07:00,187 --> 00:07:04,267
The worst one released about
thirty-nine particles a day, on average.

123
00:07:04,692 --> 00:07:05,892
That doesn't mean you are swallowing

124
00:07:06,122 --> 00:07:09,212
thirty-nine dangerous
particles every morning.

125
00:07:09,932 --> 00:07:12,072
The study did not establish that.

126
00:07:12,683 --> 00:07:15,143
It simply shows something much more basic.

127
00:07:15,923 --> 00:07:20,043
A plastic toothbrush can shed plastic
particles during ordinary use.

128
00:07:20,296 --> 00:07:23,736
And that's important because
this is not an unusual failure.

129
00:07:24,456 --> 00:07:27,816
The toothbrush is doing exactly
what it was designed to do.

130
00:07:30,486 --> 00:07:34,506
Until now, most of our examples
have been things inside a house.

131
00:07:35,086 --> 00:07:37,306
But we can make the scale much larger.

132
00:07:37,923 --> 00:07:43,063
Consider an artificial football pitch.
It looks like grass. But it isn't.

133
00:07:43,546 --> 00:07:45,286
The blades are synthetic fibres.

134
00:07:45,516 --> 00:07:48,366
And the surface is
subjected to thousands of

135
00:07:48,596 --> 00:07:51,826
footsteps, running, turning,
sliding and jumping.

136
00:07:52,583 --> 00:07:55,563
The fibres bend. They stretch. They rub.

137
00:07:55,643 --> 00:07:57,443
And they can eventually break away.

138
00:07:58,099 --> 00:08:01,259
Researchers studying
artificial turf have found that

139
00:08:01,489 --> 00:08:04,539
mechanical activity can
contribute to the release and

140
00:08:04,769 --> 00:08:08,559
movement of synthetic fibres
and other plastic materials.

141
00:08:09,031 --> 00:08:12,371
But this is also an area where
the science is still developing.

142
00:08:13,131 --> 00:08:17,411
We don't yet have a perfect number for
how much every artificial pitch releases.

143
00:08:18,303 --> 00:08:20,943
Different turf systems
use different materials.

144
00:08:21,683 --> 00:08:23,683
Different infills behave differently.

145
00:08:24,563 --> 00:08:27,923
And weathering, sunlight and
maintenance all change the result.

146
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:30,720
But the mechanism is easy to understand.

147
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:35,080
Something made from plastic is being
repeatedly rubbed against shoes.

148
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:38,240
And some of that material
can leave the pitch.

149
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:45,520
And now we can see the pattern.
A sponge. A cutting board.

150
00:08:45,750 --> 00:08:47,220
Containers in a dishwasher.

151
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:49,000
Clothes in a washing machine.

152
00:08:49,537 --> 00:08:53,697
A string trimmer. A shoe. A
toothbrush. Artificial grass.

153
00:08:54,435 --> 00:08:56,195
They don't look like the same problem.

154
00:08:56,275 --> 00:08:57,935
But they are.

155
00:08:58,135 --> 00:09:00,455
They all involve something being worn.

156
00:09:01,727 --> 00:09:05,447
And that gives us a very different way
of thinking about plastic pollution.

157
00:09:06,325 --> 00:09:08,365
We normally imagine it like this.

158
00:09:08,965 --> 00:09:14,625
A plastic object is thrown away. Then
it breaks down. Then it becomes smaller.

159
00:09:15,025 --> 00:09:17,185
Then eventually... microplastics.

160
00:09:18,034 --> 00:09:19,914
But that isn't the only pathway.

161
00:09:20,274 --> 00:09:24,954
Sometimes it looks like this.
Plastic. Friction. Wear. Particles.

162
00:09:25,771 --> 00:09:28,331
No bottle has to be thrown into a river.

163
00:09:28,871 --> 00:09:31,731
No plastic bag has to
be abandoned on a beach.

164
00:09:32,504 --> 00:09:34,184
The object can be working perfectly.

165
00:09:34,414 --> 00:09:37,664
And still lose tiny pieces of itself.

166
00:09:38,247 --> 00:09:42,127
That may be one of the strangest
things about microplastic pollution.

167
00:09:43,247 --> 00:09:47,527
The plastic doesn't always become
pollution because we used it incorrectly.

168
00:09:48,372 --> 00:09:51,972
Sometimes... pollution is the
consequence of using it correctly.

169
00:09:55,282 --> 00:09:57,602
And there is one more important point.

170
00:09:58,782 --> 00:10:00,502
Finding microplastics
does not automatically

171
00:10:00,732 --> 00:10:03,062
tell us what they are doing to our bodies.

172
00:10:03,764 --> 00:10:08,384
Just as with the tyres, we still don't
know what level of exposure is harmful.

173
00:10:08,808 --> 00:10:11,428
So this isn't a story about
a toothbrush poisoning you...

174
00:10:11,658 --> 00:10:14,888
or a cutting board
secretly making you sick.

175
00:10:15,588 --> 00:10:18,748
The science isn't there to
justify claims like that.

176
00:10:20,948 --> 00:10:23,388
It is a story about
something much simpler.

177
00:10:24,068 --> 00:10:27,408
We built a world out of materials
that are extremely durable.

178
00:10:27,707 --> 00:10:29,887
And then we made those
materials rub against things...

179
00:10:30,117 --> 00:10:33,787
wash against things... cut against
things... walk on things...

180
00:10:34,017 --> 00:10:35,227
and spin through things...

181
00:10:35,457 --> 00:10:37,067
every single day.

182
00:10:37,574 --> 00:10:40,634
With the tyres, we asked where
all that missing tread went.

183
00:10:41,174 --> 00:10:43,274
Here, the question is the same.

184
00:10:43,934 --> 00:10:46,614
Only this time, they're
things we use every day.

185
00:10:47,810 --> 00:10:52,510
Sometimes the missing material goes
somewhere we can see. And sometimes...

186
00:10:52,890 --> 00:10:55,730
it becomes so small that
we can't see it anymore.

187
00:10:56,270 --> 00:10:59,290
But becoming invisible isn't
the same as disappearing.

188
00:11:01,210 --> 00:11:03,630
And that leaves us with
a strange question.

189
00:11:04,157 --> 00:11:08,017
If some plastic pollution is created
simply by using the things around us...

190
00:11:08,097 --> 00:11:10,897
how much microplastic are we creating...

191
00:11:11,117 --> 00:11:13,037
without ever realising it?

192
00:11:15,247 --> 00:11:17,147
But now there's another question.

193
00:11:17,767 --> 00:11:20,047
If we can't stop using all these things...

194
00:11:20,127 --> 00:11:22,227
what can we actually do about it?

195
00:11:22,969 --> 00:11:24,939
That's a question for another video.
